#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c514865c-02d0-5d59-938b-fdd739cdb257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "506f95f9-9d51-59a3-a5b0-a52118bebc1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-622" :question/problem "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 10, 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 10,20\\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨10,0⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 10\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10t+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 10,20\\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨10(1)+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,20⟩ & =⟨10+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =10+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nC_2 = 20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨10t,\\,20⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "6f82e515-9c0e-5b5f-8d6d-70dc5aaa7b38" :question/math-academy-id "e-623" :question/problem "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 2e^{2t}, 3e^{3t}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2{e}^{2t},3{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{e}^{2(1)}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3(1)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{2},2{e}^{3}⟩ & =⟨{e}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=0$ and $C_2 = e^3.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{3t}+{e}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{e}^{2(2)},\\,{e}^{3(2)}+{e}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{4},\\,{e}^{6}+{e}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087528 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "ff3b5885-2f13-5c94-9619-7b0647e17037" :question/math-academy-id "e-624" :question/problem "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 1, 2t \\rangle, \\quad -\\infty <        t <        \\infty.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 2,0 \\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 2,0\\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{1}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=-1.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨t+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1, \\\\ {t}^{2}-1=(t+1)(t-1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c514865c-02d0-5d59-938b-fdd739cdb257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cab502-18f2-5c28-bb54-02e45f00569c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348094} {:answer/id #uuid "2f621e86-d8ef-5fe3-8eca-6cebec5e6248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+8),t+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348095} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1857a7-a665-59b6-95f4-ac0f6b6d59d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},4t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348096} {:answer/id #uuid "f33a0027-478b-576a-9923-5b2f75119578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348097} {:answer/id #uuid "fd19f564-179f-5de3-8c60-9e3504a5a9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34cab502-18f2-5c28-bb54-02e45f00569c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348094} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92a3946-a88f-5d59-8619-57fb5e4b430d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f2a7046-8943-5dcd-b8be-3c711d226765" :question/math-academy-id "q-122056" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},4⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},4⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{C}_{1},4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)+{C}_{1},4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{2}) \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =4+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,4t-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡[5(t+1)],4t-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d046725-244d-56d6-a8b2-df3e5a5b38ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348101} {:answer/id #uuid "56e828a1-a9e3-518f-9c98-e87be514ddb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨-6,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348102} {:answer/id #uuid "deb350f9-9ac3-5bb2-b654-eb5f67c4ad4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨4,\\frac{5}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348103} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb0d3fb-e258-5736-92cb-ecf67343e00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨-6,\\frac{5}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348104} {:answer/id #uuid "8c98b298-27ef-5635-9653-8aa22a58ac1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨4,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdb0d3fb-e258-5736-92cb-ecf67343e00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨-6,\\frac{5}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348104} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2684b09-44d1-50de-b297-a6872a770351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c014a6f8-66f2-56f8-b047-75d428488686" :question/math-academy-id "q-122093" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1,1-t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨0,1⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{t}^{2}-1,1-t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int ({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (1-t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t+{C}_{1},t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-3+{C}_{1},3-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨6+{C}_{1},-\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t-6,t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-0-6,0-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,\\frac{5}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "395d723a-b7d3-554c-8add-dee6b1b774ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨2e-4,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348108} {:answer/id #uuid "08121b8a-0638-5d89-8bdc-7e1672b3547b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨e-4\\sqrt{e},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348109} {:answer/id #uuid "58ae6935-7d73-54b0-abf7-73323bfe7558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨2e-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348110} {:answer/id #uuid "a9211c84-1a31-5f97-bcf7-5f6d289ddfac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨e-4\\sqrt{e},-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348111} {:answer/id #uuid "57dfd0eb-a2a2-5b04-ba03-b66e18790552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨e-1,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "395d723a-b7d3-554c-8add-dee6b1b774ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(0)=⟨2e-4,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348108} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71329d8-b23b-5a2f-8781-0495bd51f360" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94e287ae-0e52-5d08-b244-18ba60d3834f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122094" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨1-{e}^{t/2},3⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨0,1⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1-{e}^{t/2},3⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (1-{e}^{t/2})\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-2{e}^{t/2}+{C}_{1},3t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨2-2{e}^{2/2}+{C}_{1},3(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨2-2e+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=2e-2$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t-2{e}^{t/2}+2e-2,3t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨0-2{e}^{0/2}+2e-2,3(0)-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2e-4,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd024a9-67d7-5cea-93dc-b60e95c531f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(4)=⟨2,\\frac{9}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348115} {:answer/id #uuid "d89f293d-9e50-5677-bcfb-32e1be233eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(4)=⟨4,\\frac{13}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348116} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b39812-6e51-57e5-8185-4f841753c483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(4)=⟨1,\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348117} {:answer/id #uuid "327cbe25-f762-5c61-97c4-39db1ce5c4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(4)=⟨2,\\frac{13}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348118} {:answer/id #uuid "9b75ca5a-c4a6-5c5b-9e23-4091e45630fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(4)=⟨4,\\frac{15}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "327cbe25-f762-5c61-97c4-39db1ce5c4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(4)=⟨2,\\frac{13}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348118} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8e3392-b0fb-55df-9b5d-f5d0682c4cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8dd77c1-f4a2-57d4-8ac4-bdb67b141f30" :question/math-academy-id "q-122095" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,-1⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}},t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}+{C}_{1},\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1}}+{C}_{1},\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1⟩ & =⟨-2+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=3$ and ${C}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}+3,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}}+3,\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{13}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44202b74-22d5-5a87-81b8-dcfee4d14566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348122} {:answer/id #uuid "76eee26e-5d50-5a46-96e8-1fe5f66c8b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348123} {:answer/id #uuid "a3430732-bdc1-5b7e-a70a-c152342f01fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348124} {:answer/id #uuid "0a869793-12d5-560b-8481-d163bceeec8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348125} {:answer/id #uuid "387a411f-8e7f-59e8-87dc-db61db07fd46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44202b74-22d5-5a87-81b8-dcfee4d14566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348122} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d926ef9-f3da-5d24-9898-f016715d0241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f72a972-4df0-59a3-be02-4332225a0edb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122096" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨-1,2⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,2⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-1,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-t+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨-1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨-2+{C}_{1},2(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨-2+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨1-t,2t-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1-t,2t-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-t & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1, \\\\ 2t-2=2(t-1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07641fdd-afe6-58e9-9fca-0e138a2fb081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348129} {:answer/id #uuid "58e7bacd-3b0e-5fb9-be73-6103b6c60bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348130} {:answer/id #uuid "b5444bea-8d10-5166-8fbf-344b3c94f862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348131} {:answer/id #uuid "d1769380-a132-55a4-959b-4f7772acbfbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348132} {:answer/id #uuid "468bea99-e4bb-57fe-8ed5-6e2433f2373e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e7bacd-3b0e-5fb9-be73-6103b6c60bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348130} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aefd3f2-fe46-5c35-bd15-188b33a0aba0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd6bfe3c-713d-55d7-8194-7b0f799fe21c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122102" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨2t-6,\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{t-2}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,1⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-6,\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{t-2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-6t+{C}_{1},\\sqrt[3]{(t-2)^{4}}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨-1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨{3}^{2}-6(3)+{C}_{1},\\sqrt[3]{(3-2)^{4}}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,1⟩ & =⟨-9+{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=8$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-6t+8,\\sqrt[3]{(t-2)^{4}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{2}-6t+8,\\sqrt[3]{(t-2)^{4}}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-6t+8=(t-2)(t-4) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=2,4, \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(t-2)^{4}} & =0\\,⇒\\,t=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=2,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b675e6cc-52f0-5abd-89c3-0409e8ef6f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3) = ⟨ - 3, 4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085338} {:answer/id #uuid "c032da78-9e43-508f-83b3-f01b3812d803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3) = ⟨162,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085339} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc7e9f2-9a58-51de-a206-f70a348a29af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3) = ⟨162, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085340} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff7932f-db74-5e12-892c-34ed84466c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3) = ⟨6,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085341} {:answer/id #uuid "36d40652-85c7-56ac-9d64-25fc34c0cf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3) = ⟨159, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36d40652-85c7-56ac-9d64-25fc34c0cf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3) = ⟨159, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085342} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c585abe-a2c7-5a7c-b3d2-7bfed6113e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6b9395e-ca26-5410-b05e-eb2ce11d7f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31628" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,\\,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int -1\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨-1,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨2(1)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-3$ and ${C}_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨2{t}^{4}-3,\\,-t+4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨2(3)^{4}-3,\\,-(3)+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨159,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7a77d68-e020-5747-90cb-42e5f1065429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348136} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbd75f2-f912-5939-b3d6-dde6e3887872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348137} {:answer/id #uuid "82fdc7e5-1b2e-5945-809f-af9099718768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348138} {:answer/id #uuid "4d42ae05-6daf-571b-8502-d0a8b9011be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348139} {:answer/id #uuid "1f901f0a-fcac-579d-a075-6cce445c0249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82fdc7e5-1b2e-5945-809f-af9099718768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348138} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e19ed7-69f5-5c50-a7e4-105d584ce353" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1d2200e-dbc1-5e87-951b-4fac16df86f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31634" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨3{t}^{2}-10t,2t-4⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-18,-8⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-10t,2t-4⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-10t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (2t-4)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}-4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨-18,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨(3)^{3}-5(3)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(3)^{2}-4(3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-18,-8⟩ & =⟨-18+{C}_{1},\\,-3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-4t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-4t-5⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}={t}^{2}(t-5) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,5, \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5=(t+1)(t-5) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=5,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c514865c-02d0-5d59-938b-fdd739cdb257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e80a47-07bf-5eb4-adb0-fa314a8b7085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan(t)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4e80a47-07bf-5eb4-adb0-fa314a8b7085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan(t)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348143} :answer-field/id #uuid "21752fd0-7af3-5d06-b583-61d3e3457f8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348142} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "885f72e8-f347-51bc-927b-a2a82496c29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sec(t)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "885f72e8-f347-51bc-927b-a2a82496c29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sec(t)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348145} :answer-field/id #uuid "79b4abb3-54b7-5643-9900-4881cfb2155a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e049daf-f844-5c35-987e-fb3bc83131a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-351063" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3{sec}^{2}⁡(t),\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨2,\\,1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{sec}^{2}⁡(t),\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3{sec}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int -2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)+{C}_{1},-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)+{C}_{1},-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ={C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-2+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)+2,\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+3⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c514865c-02d0-5d59-938b-fdd739cdb257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "534b0141-590b-55f0-be64-d7ee4d209642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t\\sqrt{t}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "534b0141-590b-55f0-be64-d7ee4d209642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t\\sqrt{t}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348148} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62cf4a6-e02e-58ce-a91e-eece38f6cccb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aec7757f-ba50-550b-9a62-8ed2cf77669d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-8\\sqrt{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aec7757f-ba50-550b-9a62-8ed2cf77669d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-8\\sqrt{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348150} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e7c8c57-4581-5f6e-8aaf-dffbea8f4593" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "878070eb-86bc-5f13-93e4-3c9b1d034520" :question/math-academy-id "q-351064" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨2,\\,-1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\sqrt{t}+{C}_{1},-8\\sqrt{t+1}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\sqrt{0}+{C}_{1},-8\\sqrt{0+1}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-8+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ={C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =-8+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t\\sqrt{t}+2,\\,-8\\sqrt{t+1}+7⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d8584f3-cfeb-5f5e-bff4-1965b9f69633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d8584f3-cfeb-5f5e-bff4-1965b9f69633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348153} :answer-field/id #uuid "de03e589-e6f7-5b8a-935e-30a205008e5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa28f0b6-43f4-519c-8e63-f65e230e5540" :question/math-academy-id "q-351102" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3\\sqrt{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,\\,-14⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary when $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{1/2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{3/2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨-1,\\,-14⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{1}^{1/2}+{C}_{1},\\,2\\cdot {1}^{3/2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,\\,-14⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=-16.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{1/2}-2,\\,2{t}^{3/2}-16⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{1/2}-2,\\,2{t}^{3/2}-16⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{1/2}-2=0 & \\,⇒\\,{t}^{1/2}=2\\,⇒\\,t=4, \\\\ 2{t}^{3/2}-16=0 & \\,⇒\\,{t}^{3/2}=8\\,⇒\\,t=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=4.$ So, this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c514865c-02d0-5d59-938b-fdd739cdb257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b888a901-15ec-559f-b51a-bd643290dd2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v = ⟨\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} - \\frac{3}{2}e^{2}, \\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} + \\frac{2}{3}e^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085808} {:answer/id #uuid "d082f772-9e40-52a6-9169-e6fc31f3c702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v = ⟨\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} + \\frac{3}{2}e^{2}, \\frac{2}{3}e^{3t} + \\frac{2}{3}e^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085809} {:answer/id #uuid "fa181cf2-4bd3-533e-b90b-8bbdfedbdaf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v = ⟨\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} + \\frac{1}{2}e^{2}, \\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} + \\frac{2}{3}e^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085810} {:answer/id #uuid "ff77f671-4a21-5d88-bcd8-f90f35438a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v = ⟨\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} - \\frac{1}{2}e^{2}, \\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} - \\frac{2}{3}e^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085811} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6ac3e5-243c-5870-bf59-fc859daef79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v = ⟨\\frac{3}{2}e^{2t} + \\frac{3}{2}e^{2}, \\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} + \\frac{2}{3}e^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa181cf2-4bd3-533e-b90b-8bbdfedbdaf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v = ⟨\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} + \\frac{1}{2}e^{2}, \\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} + \\frac{2}{3}e^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085810} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbdb9be6-df45-538c-ac45-f937c24d4d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e2b1a45-a91f-5853-8607-c70b94477f9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-51343" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c514865c-02d0-5d59-938b-fdd739cdb257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "705217b9-24a0-5af1-8b92-c97f48c99a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨2t+4,4t+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348156} {:answer/id #uuid "53073877-5eee-5f9c-80d2-d2f9bdc5c639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348157} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef3686b-7f46-518f-8c55-97f999468d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨3t+1,4t+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348158} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfac762-e029-56df-9463-29ee57060106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨t+4,2t+3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348159} {:answer/id #uuid "14c9993d-474e-5a0b-9cce-19cf9104d686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨4t+3,3t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53073877-5eee-5f9c-80d2-d2f9bdc5c639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348157} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd5e6c7-d12e-5f09-88d5-a6ab1849f804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3c2a344-d3bb-5ceb-a76b-4fdb148ea6a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-7196" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨1,2⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨4,3⟩$ when $t=1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨4,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1+{C}_{1},2(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,3⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =2+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc07113-45ae-57ab-a1e7-3807d4d7762b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨121,15⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348163} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1c76f1-5865-5bb5-b472-385c94e18e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨32,-49⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348164} {:answer/id #uuid "65405267-a774-58c1-8aea-b692c07e61fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨85,-8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348165} {:answer/id #uuid "df7fc3fc-27b9-54ea-9ed9-2f6356719d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-66,21⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348166} {:answer/id #uuid "82374c58-fdf5-5301-b030-3846b897f32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨112,10⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bc07113-45ae-57ab-a1e7-3807d4d7762b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨121,15⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348163} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae94ca7-9104-5d44-aba1-a4d8f1ee253f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb1ed2a5-10d4-520a-8be1-03271b56ffd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-83497" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9(1-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${C}_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(1))^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3(1))^{3}+7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨121,15⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29103b4f-d3d0-5020-87fa-8fb737927132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348170} {:answer/id #uuid "650c528f-b33c-5c51-9344-4a81fb1caccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348171} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8acd59-52cc-5f76-b014-36556bb4c524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348172} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec5ccb1-7736-5466-998d-b783bc71b510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348173} {:answer/id #uuid "d21a7d16-6de2-59bb-b589-c98a3591c425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "650c528f-b33c-5c51-9344-4a81fb1caccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348171} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af7f42d-8cdf-5cd9-84e2-0449e0b8129d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6b776df-4161-52d4-97da-6b0310ac19a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-83506" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩,\\,0\\le t<\\pi .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,1⟩$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=⟨1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=0,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}]}
